const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do I write an ISO 9001 document control procedure?',
    a: 'Start from the outcome clause 7.5.3 asks for: decide how documents are identified and numbered, who reviews and approves them, how they’re issued and revised, and how obsolete versions are removed from use. The worked example on this page covers each of those sections in plain language you can adapt.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What should an ISO 9001 document control procedure example include?',
    a: 'A complete example covers purpose and scope, who’s responsible for what, a numbering scheme, how documents are created, reviewed and approved, how they’re issued and accessed, how changes and revisions are handled, how external documents and obsolete documents are controlled, and how long records are retained.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to number every document?',
    a: 'You need a way to uniquely identify each document and tell its current version from a previous one. A numbering and revision scheme is the simplest way to do that consistently. It doesn’t need to be elaborate. A short prefix by document type plus a sequence number and a revision number, like QP-001 Rev 3, is enough for most small companies.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do you prevent unintended use of obsolete documents?',
    a: 'Remove the previous version from anywhere it’s actively used as soon as the new one is issued. Take down the printed copy, replace the file on the shared drive, or, in a system like Obel-MS, let approval automatically supersede the old version. Keep the obsolete version on file for your retention period, but mark it clearly as no longer current.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do supplier certificates count as external documents under ISO 9001?',
    a: 'Yes. A supplier certificate of analysis, safety data sheet or accreditation certificate is documented information you didn’t create yourself, so it falls under clause 7.5.3.2’s control of documents of external origin. Log it, know its current version, and track when it needs renewing, the same as any document you wrote in-house.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long should you keep superseded document versions?',
    a: 'ISO 9001 doesn’t set a fixed retention period, in either the 2015 or 2026 edition. It asks you to decide one and follow it. Many small companies keep at least the previous two or three revisions, or the last few years, whichever is more useful for tracing what was in effect when something happened. State the period in your procedure and apply it consistently.',
  },
];
