>
  <h2>Do you need a documented procedure for document control?</h2>
  <p>
    No. ISO 9001:2015 dropped "control of documents" from its list of mandatory documented procedures, and ISO 9001:2026, the
    standard&rsquo;s sixth edition published in September 2026, carries this forward unchanged, so nothing in either
    current edition forces you to write one. What clause 7.5.3 does require is the outcome: documented information
    that&rsquo;s properly identified, reviewed, approved, controlled and eventually disposed of. Most small and mid-sized
    companies write a short procedure anyway. It&rsquo;s the fastest way to get
    everyone doing document control the same way, and the easiest thing to hand an auditor who asks how it works.
  </p>
  <p>
    A written procedure also protects you from the most common failure mode in a small company: one person knowing how document
    control works, and nobody else being sure. If that person is out sick during an audit, or leaves the business altogether,
    a short procedure means the next person can pick it up without reverse-engineering it from memory.
  </p>

  <h2>What should a document control procedure include?</h2>
  <p>
    A complete procedure answers a small, fixed set of questions: who&rsquo;s responsible, how documents are identified, how
    they move from draft to approved, how people access the current version, how changes happen, how you handle documents you
    didn&rsquo;t write yourself, what happens to old versions, and how long records are kept. The worked example below covers
    each of those in order, written for a fictional small manufacturer. Adapt the names, numbers and reviewers to your
    own business, but the structure will fit most companies preparing for a first ISO 9001 certification or tidying up before an
    audit.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example: document control procedure</h2>
  <p>
    <em>This is an example. Replace the job titles, numbering scheme and retention periods
    with your own before you use it.</em>
  </p>

  <h3>1. Purpose</h3>
  <p>
    This procedure describes how [Company Name] creates, reviews, approves, issues, revises and retires the documented
    information used in its quality management system, and how it controls documents of external origin.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Scope</h3>
  <p>
    This procedure applies to all quality procedures, work instructions, forms, registers and external documents used within
    the QMS. It does not apply to general correspondence, marketing material or documents outside the scope of ISO 9001.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Responsibilities</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Document owner</strong>: drafts and maintains the document, and initiates any revision.</li>
    <li><strong>Reviewer</strong>: a person competent in the subject matter, checks the document is technically correct and workable before approval.</li>
    <li><strong>Approver</strong>: typically the quality manager or department head, signs off the document before release.</li>
    <li><strong>Quality manager</strong>: maintains the document register, the numbering scheme and the external document list, and confirms obsolete versions are withdrawn.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Definitions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Controlled document</strong>: a document managed through this procedure, with one current, approved version.</li>
    <li><strong>Uncontrolled copy</strong>: a copy, such as a printout, that is not automatically updated when the source document changes.</li>
    <li><strong>External document</strong>: documented information [Company Name] relies on but did not author, such as a standard, a customer specification or a supplier certificate.</li>
    <li><strong>Obsolete document</strong>: a document or version that has been superseded and is no longer valid for use.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5. Document identification and numbering</h3>
  <p>
    Every controlled document gets a unique reference made up of a two- or three-letter type prefix, a three-digit sequence
    number, and a revision number, for example <strong>QP-001 Rev 3</strong>: quality procedure number one, third revision.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Prefix</th>
          <th>Document type</th>
          <th>Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {prefixes.map((p) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{p.prefix}</td>
            <td>{p.meaning}</td>
            <td class="tnum">{p.example}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The quality manager assigns the next available sequence number when a new document is created, and keeps a register mapping
    each reference to its title, owner, current revision and approval date.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep the scheme small enough that people can read a reference and understand it without looking anything up. A five-person
    company rarely needs more than three or four prefixes. Adding a new one for every department or process usually creates more
    confusion than it solves. If you already run a numbering scheme, whatever it is, this procedure only needs to describe it
    accurately. There&rsquo;s no requirement to match the example above.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Creation and review</h3>
  <p>
    The document owner drafts the document using the standard template for its type, and gives it a working title and a
    provisional reference number. Before it goes for approval, a reviewer with relevant competence checks it for technical
    accuracy, clarity and fit with how the work is done. A procedure nobody can follow in practice is a finding
    waiting to happen.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Approval</h3>
  <p>
    The approver named for that document type reviews it for suitability and adequacy and signs off, recording their name and
    the date. A document has no standing under this procedure until it has been approved. Draft versions are marked "Draft" and
    are not issued for use.
  </p>

  <h3>8. Issue and access</h3>
  <p>
    Once approved, the quality manager issues the current version to the people who need it and updates the register. Where
    documents are held in a shared system, access is controlled by role so that only the document owner and quality manager can
    edit the source, while everyone who needs it can view the current version. Printed copies are marked "uncontrolled when
    printed" so nobody mistakes one for the current version by default.
  </p>

  <h3>9. Changes and revisions</h3>
  <p>
    Anyone can request a change, but only the document owner initiates a revision. A revised document goes through the same
    review and approval steps as a new one, and its revision number increases by one. The previous version is retained on file,
    marked obsolete, and withdrawn from anywhere it was in active use.
  </p>

  <h3>10. Documents of external origin</h3>
  <p>
    Standards, customer specifications, regulations and supplier certificates such as certificates of analysis or safety data
    sheets are logged on a separate external document register, with the supplier or issuing body, the current issue or
    expiry date, and an owner responsible for checking it&rsquo;s still current. [Company Name] does not edit external
    documents, but controls which version is in use and when it needs replacing, the same way it controls its own documents.
  </p>

  <h3>11. Obsolete documents</h3>
  <p>
    When a document is superseded, the previous version is removed from active use immediately and either archived with a clear
    "obsolete" marking or, where it&rsquo;s retained purely for legal or knowledge purposes, filed separately from current
    documents so it cannot be mistaken for one.
  </p>

  <h3>12. Records retention</h3>
  <p>
    Records created under this procedure, such as the document register and approval history, are retained for [state period,
    for example three years or the life of the product] and are then disposed of securely. External documents such as expired
    supplier certificates are retained for [state period] after they lapse, to show what was in effect at the time.
  </p>

  <h3>13. Related documents</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Nonconformance and corrective action procedure</li>
    <li>Internal audit procedure</li>
    <li>Management review procedure</li>
    <li>Document register / external document register</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How do you adapt this example for your own company?</h2>
  <p>
    Replace every bracketed placeholder with your own detail, keep the section order (an auditor who has seen dozens of these
    procedures will recognise the shape), and cut anything that doesn&rsquo;t apply to how you work. A five-person
    company rarely needs a separate reviewer and approver for every document type. The one thing worth keeping no matter how you
    trim it is a clear answer to the two questions an audit always comes back to: how do you know this is the current version,
    and how do you know the old one is out of use. See our <a href="/guides/iso-9001-document-control">guide to ISO 9001 document
    control requirements</a> for how each section above maps back to clause 7.5.
  </p>
  <p>
    It&rsquo;s worth reviewing the procedure itself once a year, or whenever it stops matching how you work. A
    procedure that describes a process nobody follows any more is its own kind of nonconformance. Treat that review the same way
    you&rsquo;d treat a revision to any other document: someone owns it, someone checks it, and the change goes through approval
    before it becomes the new current version.
  </p>
